I read the whole log. I’m staying put. Here’s the basket, then my view on your open questions, then one thing to do next.

The basket

One idea runs through all of it. Each workbench (the Armory for guns, CarMaker for cars, the C12 builder for buildings, roads and terrain, the 2M/2F rigs for characters) does the hard math once and exports a .glb that carries its own physics through KHR_physics_rigid_bodies and KHR_implicit_shapes. The game itself stays small: Rapier, ecctrl, and one shared loader that reads any of those files. That loader is the “shared import library” you asked for, and nobody in that log built it cleanly as its own thing.
Each workbench has a “Kolmogorov” fixed point, the one rule everything snaps to. Guns: the 21.2 mm rail plus the palm IK points. Cars: the wheel positions. Buildings: the 3.0 m C12 lattice of squares and triangles at 30° steps. Characters: 4 shared skeletons, with clothing turned on and off by a bitmask.
Parked, but kept: the terrain/road/curb coupling where each building gets one floor height and foundation skirts drop to the ground, exact-integer lattice coordinates, PSX paper walls vs. 0.25 m framed walls, the 4-band trim sheet, and the Tommy gun with the cartoon drum mag.
Real traps the reviews caught, which I agree with: list the physics extensions in extensionsUsed only, never in extensionsRequired. Don’t flip the forward axis with negative scale. Never use zero-thickness colliders. Don’t rig car wheels in KHR and in ShapeCastWheel at the same time; tag the wheel nodes and let ecctrl own them.
Your questions

Should you use KHR at all? Yes. Putting the physics inside a standard glTF extension, instead of a homemade sidecar file, is the right call, and it’s the reason any workbench can feed any game. As far as I know the extension is still a Khronos draft, so its schema can still shift. Keep your loader’s reading code in one file so a schema change is a one-file fix.

Should doors be real Rapier physics, or just move from A to B? Mostly A to B, but with a collider that moves along. Make the door a kinematic body and drive its angle with damped interpolation. Players can’t walk through it and it can’t explode. Use real dynamic joints with motors only where the physics is the fun part: a car door flapping open in a crash, the jack-in-the-box. For a gun in your hands, Gemini was right: animate the parts as plain transforms with no physics bodies, and only make it physical once it’s dropped in the world. The same KHR data (hinge axis and limits) drives all three cases.

