I read both. Here’s what they actually say, in plain terms.

The docs (api-reference.md)

A car is <EcctrlVehicle> with <ShapeCastWheel>s inside it. Wheels find their car by themselves, so you can have any number of them.
EcctrlVehicle is a normal Rapier rigid body, so you put your own colliders and mesh inside it.
One rule matters a lot: the default numbers assume normal weight, around density={1}. If the car is heavy, suspension, damping, engine power and brakes all have to be scaled up with it.
The docs say they cover ecctrl@2.0.0 and import from "ecctrl/vehicle". Your fork is named @pinkaphant/ecctrl, so your imports would be "@pinkaphant/ecctrl/vehicle".
The example (EcctrlWrapper.tsx, 1,036 lines)

Most of that file is Leva tuning sliders and enter/exit logic. The car itself is about 25 lines:

tsx
<EcctrlVehicle ref={vehicle1Ref} position={[5,2,-60]} carConfig={{ engineHorsepower: 600, ... }}>
  <CuboidCollider args={[1, 0.4, 2.4]} position={[0, 0.1, 0]} density={200} />   // body box
  <mesh geometry={VehicleBody1} position={[0, 0.1, 0]} />                         // body look

  <ShapeCastWheel steerWheel driveWheel brakeWheel position={[ 0.9, 0,  1.8]}>   // front left
    <mesh geometry={R05Wheel} rotation={[0, 0, Math.PI/2]} />
  </ShapeCastWheel>
  ... same at [-0.9, 0, 1.8] (front right)
  ... driveWheel brakeWheel at [±0.9, 0, -1.8] (rears, no steer)

  <CylinderCollider sensor mass={0} onIntersectionEnter=... />                   // "press E to enter"
</EcctrlVehicle>
Wheels go at (±0.9, 0, ±1.8). The front wheels steer, all four drive and brake.
The wheel radius is 0.5 everywhere: the cast shape (rayShapeR) and the visual wheel (wheelModelRadius) match.
The body uses density={200}, so the wheels use springK 38000 and dampingC 4000 instead of the defaults of 180 and 16. That’s the “scale with weight” rule in practice.
Car 2 is the same pattern plus a second body box, rear driveTorqueWeight={2}, and different front and rear brake torque.
Driving is one call every frame: setMovement({ forward, backward, steerLeft, steerRight, brake }). The camera follows currPos.
Getting in uses a sensor collider that checks whether the player’s collider name is "character-capsule-collider".
The models come from one file, vehicles.glb, with separate body and wheel meshes.
So a car really is: one body box, one body mesh, four wheel positions with flags, one radius, and spring settings matched to the weight. That’s the whole thing.

